			<div class="content" id="post-content-3893504">
									

<p>Oh boy, I was fearing it'd turn out like this. I know the collapsible is too dry, as pointed by you and scpcrnp below, and I really considered just removing it. In the end, I left it for the benefit (?) of people not acquainted with classical logic (mostly for the examples), so I guess it definitely comes out as a textbook (and not a very well-written one).</p>
<p>Maybe I should just move the most relevant part (which would be the last paragraph) to the description and remove the rest, move it to a separate supplement page or make it clear it can be skipped, as the bulk of the story itself is in Addendum 1, and the knowledge required is that &quot;SCP-4xxx is an anomalously persuasive argumentation scheme&quot; and nothing else. Would something on the lines of &quot;Researchers acquainted with the basics of symbolic logic may skip this section&quot; work?<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-144339-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-144339-1')">1</a></sup>&#8230; however, I am aware that the dryness detracts from the rest of the piece, so maybe just axing it entirely would be for the better.</p>
<p>But well, it is at +7 so I suppose I didn't entirely fail at what I set up to do, which is good (better than what I expected, even). This also gives me some extra time to think it over and see what can be changed for the SCP to be less&#8230; boring. Thank you for the honest critique, it has been quite helpful.</p>
