<p>I feel like there's too much going on at once. There's</p>
<ul>
<li>the proof itself (I agree with other commenters that the explanation collapsible could be much shorter)</li>
<li>the story of Germaine deducing the existence of 4000 (which unfortunately, much of her notes is spent describing what you already summarized in the text)</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>this was later corroborated by experimentation on Germain's part, by sending purposefully incorrect proofs (usually of nonsensical statements) to several prominent mathematicians such as Joseph-Louis Lagrange and Adrien-Marie Legendre, under various pseudonyms. While a few of the replies received pointed out that there might be an error in her work, none of them pointed to the SCP-4000 proof as faulty, instead citing false assumptions or mentioning potential new theories to explain the unexpected results.</p>
</blockquote>
<ul>
<li>the story of her saving Gauss from Napoleon</li>
<li>and then the list of other organizations attempting to exploit 4000 (which comes and goes like *that*, too abruptly to have any impact).</li>
</ul>
<p>It's great to see another math-y skip but i think for this one you might need to choose a central focus and give the overall piece a pillar to lean on.</p>
<p>-1 for now, but I do hope you revise this some more.</p>

