<p>On one hand, I like Germain's letters, and the implications she uses some anomalous logic to alter the path of Napoleon's conquest. Additionally, the fact that multiple governments are rolling out their own diet coke versions of this anomaly is quite chilling.</p>
<p>On the other hand, that bone-dry logic lesson in the middle of the document just kills any and all sense of pacing you had going into the article, and cause to really limp towards the finish.</p>
<p>I'm going to err on the side of an upvote, since the parts I liked were quite good. However, I don't think this will take of in any appreciable fashion until you trim down the lesson to keep what is designed to be entertainment, well, entertaining.</p>

